    "summary": "WARDOGS gameplay centers on a 100-player three-squad tactical FPS built around a persistent cash economy. Three squads of roughly 33 players each race inside a Control Zone, earning cash for kills, vehicle use and objective ticks. Cash carries across matches to buy deployable...",
    "hero": {
      "subtitle": "WARDOGS gameplay centers on a 100-player three-squad tactical FPS built around a persistent cash economy. Three squads of roughly 33 players each race inside a Control Zone, earning cash for kills, vehicle use and objective ticks. Cash carries across matches to buy deployable roles like ghillie suit sniper, arms dealer, helicopter pilot, artillery, builder and medic, plus vehicles. Destructible and buildable environments plus a Hot Zone bonus round out the loop.\n\n## WARDOGS gameplay loop at a glance\n\nThe core loop of WARDOGS gameplay runs across four beats per match and across matches for the season. The match beat is: deploy into a 2x2km Control Zone as one of three squads, accumulate score toward the 100-point race, earn cash on kills and objective ticks, and either win the race or be eliminated. The cross-match beat is: spend that cash on deployable roles and vehicles for the next deployment, then carry the remainder into the following match. That four-beat loop is what the Steam store page and BULKHD's \"Early Access & Beyond\" reveal describe as the 100-player three-squad tactical FPS pitch.\n\nEurogamer pre-launch coverage confirms the loop is built on a King-of-Area-style objective race with persistent cash retention, while the BULKHD reveal adds the deployable-role layer on top. The result is a hybrid: it is not a pure extraction shooter, not a pure battle royale, and not a pure objective push. It is a long-form tactical round where cash and equipment decisions between deployments matter as much as kills inside the zone.\n\n## Combat, cash and deployable roles\n\nInside the Control Zone, kills and objective ticks reward cash that carries over to the next match. That cross-match cash retention is the bridge between the combat layer and the role layer: a squad that survives a deployment with cash can re-deploy with a stronger role kit, a vehicle or a deployable turret. A squad that bleeds cash will queue into the next round with starter weapons and a longer climb back.\n\nThe deployable roles are the tactical differentiator for WARDOGS gameplay. Confirmed launch roles include the ghillie suit sniper (long-range overwatch), the arms dealer (sells weapons and equipment to squadmates), the supply-ferry helicopter (logistics and repositioning), the artillery role (long-range fire support), the builder role (Fortify key locations and forward operating bases) and the medic role (revive and sustain). Each role changes both the squad's optimal tactics and the player's personal decision-making during a match, which is why a single role draft can swing a round.\n\n## Destructible and buildable environments\n\nThe Combat Zone maps are not static boxes. Rocket launchers can take down apartment buildings, tanks can push through town blocks, Fortify can lock down key sightlines and forward operating bases can extend a squad's effective depth. That destructible-and-buildable layer is what lets a single round shift from long-range overwatch to close-quarters urban combat inside a few minutes, and it is also why deployable roles like ghillie suit sniper and builder are positioned as round-swingers rather than flavour picks.\n\nThe Hot Zone bonus is the economy hook tied to the environment layer. When a sub-zone is flagged as a Hot Zone, it pays out double cash on each scoring tick, which means a squad that can hold or contest a Hot Zone accelerates its cash curve faster than a squad that plays the wider zone. That makes environment control \u2014 not just kills \u2014 the dominant economic action inside the loop.\n\n## Squad coordination, voice and proximity\n\nWARDOGS gameplay includes Proximity voice chat, so players hear teammates and nearby enemies based on in-game distance rather than through a global channel. That voice model is what makes three-squad coordination workable at 100 players: a squad can coordinate locally without flooding the channel for the rest of the server. Combined with the squad-level deployable roles, Proximity voice gives WARDOGS a much more squad-driven feel than a generic lobby shooter.\n\nThe practical implication is that squad composition matters more than personal skill curve. A squad with a sniper, a builder, an arms dealer and a helicopter pilot covers long-range overwatch, base-building, equipment sustain and logistics in one composition. A squad that queues four of the same role will likely struggle to control a Hot Zone even with strong individual aim.\n\n## Hardcore mode as a gameplay variant\n\nWARDOGS also ships with a Hardcore mode that adjusts the standard loop rather than replacing it. Hardcore removes killcam, simplifies the HUD, limits the kill feed to the player's own squad and forces vehicles into first-person camera only. That makes Hardcore a tighter, more squad-internal information environment than the standard mode. Hardcore is not a separate game; it is a ruleset applied to the same Control Zone map pool and the same cash economy.",
      "ctas": []
    },
