    "h1": "WARDOGS Control Zone: 2x2km Three-Squad Mode Explained",
    "seoTitle": "WARDOGS Control Zone: 2x2km Three-Squad Mode Explained",
    "metaDescription": "WARDOGS Control Zone is the 2x2km three-squad mode with a 100-point race, 30-second scoring ticks and Hot Zone bonus cash. How scoring, squad split and victory work.",
    "summary": "WARDOGS Control Zone is the launch game mode: a 2x2km random sub-zone of the map with three squads of roughly 33 players each, racing to 100 points. Score ticks every 30 seconds for the squad holding the zone, and any Hot Zone inside it pays double cash. The first squad to rea...",
    "hero": {
      "subtitle": "WARDOGS Control Zone is the launch game mode: a 2x2km random sub-zone of the map with three squads of roughly 33 players each, racing to 100 points. Score ticks every 30 seconds for the squad holding the zone, and any Hot Zone inside it pays double cash. The first squad to reach 100 points wins the round. Sourced from the Steam store page and the BULKHD Early Access reveal.\n\n## WARDOGS Control Zone rules at a glance\n\nThe WARDOGS Control Zone mode runs on a small footprint rather than a full-map domination round. Each match carves a 2x2km random sub-zone out of the larger map and the three squads fight inside that box. With 100 players split across three squads of roughly 33 each, the mode reads more like a focused tactical push than a sprawling open-field battle. That footprint choice is what lets a single round resolve in tens of minutes rather than running open-ended.\n\nVictory goes to the first squad that hits 100 points, not to the squad with the most kills or the longest zone time. The point race is the only win condition, which means squad-level decisions on whether to push, hold or rotate a sub-zone all flow back to the 100-point target. Cash rewards inside the zone are tied to scoring ticks, so the economic incentive and the win-condition incentive point the same direction.\n\n## How the 100-point race and 30-second scoring work\n\nThe scoring loop is a 30-second tick. Each tick, the squad currently holding the Control Zone gains a fixed point award. A squad that can stay on the zone therefore picks up roughly two points per minute, which means the 100-point race normally resolves within the early-to-mid round if one squad can deny the others from rotating in.\n\nA squad that loses the zone stops scoring but does not lose points already banked, so the race is not a single-life run; it is a held-zone score. That distinguishes WARDOGS Control Zone from extraction-style modes where dying empties the wallet. The economic loss in WARDOGS comes from losing the zone (no more ticks) and from buying deployable roles and vehicles between deployments, not from per-death deductions.\n\nCash payouts from the scoring ticks are the primary funding source for deployable roles and vehicles in the next deployment. Squads that hold the zone longer therefore deploy into the next round with more kit, which compounds over the match. Over a long round, that compounding is what tilts a control-zone stalemate rather than raw per-tick point output.\n\n## Hot Zone bonus and squad-level tactics\n\nInside the Control Zone, BULKHD layers a Hot Zone sub-mechanic on top of the main 30-second ticks. A Hot Zone is a smaller area inside the 2x2km box that pays out double cash when scoring ticks fire while a squad is holding it. The Hot Zone is therefore the cash economy's high-leverage objective within the zone, even though it is not the only way to score.\n\nThe tactical implication is that squads should treat Hot Zone control as a separate beat from general zone control. A squad can hold the wider Control Zone for steady 30-second ticks while a sub-element of the squad contests the Hot Zone for the cash multiplier. The two objectives overlap geographically, but they reward different actions: zone time rewards survival and area denial, Hot Zone time rewards cash generation for the next deployment.\n\nThe cash multiplier also feeds into the role draft. Squads that expect to fight for the Hot Zone benefit more from deployable roles that can hold ground (builder, medic, helicopter logistics), while squads that plan to deny the zone from range benefit more from ghillie suit sniper and artillery roles. That role-versus-objective interaction is what makes WARDOGS Control Zone a tactical mode rather than a generic King-of-the-Hill.\n\n## Distinction from traditional King-of-the-Hill\n\nWARDOGS Control Zone draws its inspiration from ARMA King-of-the-Hill modes but layers in three squad-level changes: 100-player three-squad composition, persistent cash economy, and Hot Zone bonus cash. Traditional ARMA KOTH usually runs with two teams on a single static zone and no persistent economy; WARDOGS scales up the team count and re-tools the economy so cash carries across deployments.\n\nThe cash and role layer is the bigger departure. In a traditional KOTH, holding the zone is the only meaningful action and you reset to loadouts between rounds. In WARDOGS Control Zone, holding the zone funds your next deployment, which means a winning squad compounds advantage across deployments rather than starting each round from the same baseline. That compounding is what the persistent cash economy is designed to enable.\n\nThe 2x2km random sub-zone is the third difference. Most KOTH variants run on a fixed zone position. WARDOGS randomises the sub-zone inside the larger map, which forces squads to plan around a moving objective rather than memorise a single position. Combined with destructible and buildable environments, that randomisation means no two Control Zone rounds feel identical even on the same map.",
      "ctas": []
    },
